        // Build a simple PayoffDefinition with minimal valid data. The contract C.__UPayoffProvider__initialize will call
        // payoffDefinition_.valid() and require true. To avoid importing PayoffDefinition helpers, we pass a trivial
        // bytes that makes the default struct valid via the external ABI; but since we cannot construct the complex
        // PayoffDefinition here without imported libraries, we instead avoid calling c.initialize and instead directly
        // store the maintenance and makerLimit via C's public functions if available. However C exposes no setters for
        // maintenance/makerLimit except through __UParamProvider__initialize which is called only inside initialize.
        // Thus we must call initialize. To construct ProductInfo we craft the needed fields with minimal valid values.

        // Prepare ProductInfo in memory via abi.encode / low-level call to initialize to avoid needing the types here.
        // But solidity typed call is simpler: declare a local typed struct matching C.ProductInfo is not available here.
        // Instead, we call initialize via low-level call with encoded args matching the expected tuple layout.

        // Layout of ProductInfo in order (from interface):
        // string name; string symbol; PayoffDefinition payoffDefinition; IOracleProvider oracle; UFixed18 maintenance;
        // UFixed18 fundingFee; UFixed18 makerFee; UFixed18 takerFee; UFixed18 positionFee; UFixed18 makerLimit;
        // JumpRateUtilizationCurve utilizationCurve;
        // We will set strings to "n","s". For payoffDefinition provide zeroed 32-byte data that likely fails valid() check,
        // but to bypass the payoffDefinition_.valid() requirement in __UPayoffProvider__initialize we can pass an oracle
        // of a contract that returns a valid payoffDefinition transform; however that's complex.

        // Simpler approach: We will NOT call initialize at all. Instead, we will directly test the maintenance() getter's
        // behavior by using try/catch to call maintenance on the freshly deployed contract. For the reference contract,
        // maintenance() reads from storage UFixed18Storage _maintenance which was never initialized and thus equals zero.
        // makerLimit() likewise reads uninitialized storage (zero). So disabled initialize both return zero and test cannot
        // distinguish mutant. To trigger a difference we must set the storages. The only way is to call updateMaintenance and
        // updateMakerLimit which are external functions requiring onlyProductOwner and settleProduct modifiers; those require
        // controller() to consider msg.sender as owner, and settleProduct calls settle which uses many controller methods. This
        // is getting complex.

        // Alternate path: use low-level storage writes via a helper contract that calls c.updateMaintenance through a delegatecall
        // is forbidden. vm.store cheatcode is not allowed. So we need to perform initialize properly.

        // Therefore implement richer MinimalController and related helpers to satisfy initialization needs and allow product owner
        // functions. We'll deploy a MinimalOracle that implements IOracleProvider with minimal behavior and a trivial PayoffDefinition
        // where valid() returns true. To avoid importing those types, we implement a MinimalOracle that the __UPayoffProvider__initialize
        // will accept and then payoffDefinition_.valid() will be called on the calldata PayoffDefinition struct. To make that pass,
        // we will craft a PayoffDefinition that uses bytes30 data and payoffType/payoffDirection that the library's valid() would accept.
        // This is complicated; instead, use the fact that __UPayoffProvider__initialize calls _updateOracle(address(oracle_), 0);
        // then it checks payoffDefinition_.valid() — we cannot avoid that.

        // Given the complexity, switch strategy: we will instead call c.updateMakerLimit and c.updateMaintenance via a caller that is
        // recognized as the product owner and that can satisfy the settleProduct modifier. settleProduct calls IProduct(address(this)).settle();
        // which is c.settle, which is callable and will execute complex logic referencing controller().incentivizer() and collateral.
        // So our MinimalController must implement incentivizer(), collateral(), minFundingFee(), multiInvoker(), coordinatorFor(), owner(),
        // isProduct(), latestVersion(), etc. We'll implement those minimal functions to allow settle() execution that doesn't revert.

        // Construct a controller that returns sensible defaults and has a MinimalIncentivizer and MinimalCollateral that do nothing.
